{
  "rationale": "Iteration 0 raced all 60 trials and every run ended off track, with one line-loss event on each track and max_error of 0.062 to 0.065 m. That is just past the 0.06 m off-track limit, so the robot is drifting off the line rather than stalling. Steering oscillation is moderate (0.19 to 0.25), so the body is not the main problem. Slalom progress is only 0.14 and s-bend 0.27, which fits a robot that loses the line on the first sharp bend and then leaves the track. The body cannot buy more margin: the limit is max(0.06, 0.0125 + body_width/4), and even the widest allowed body (0.18 m) gives 0.0575 m, so the 0.06 m floor stays. The fix has to come from sensing. Widening the row from ±0.04 m to ±0.06 m gives the outer sensors reach past the tape edge on sharp curves, which should reduce the line-loss events that start each off-track failure. I am also enlarging the sensor footprint from 12 mm to 16 mm so each sensor keeps a non-zero reading while the tape is near its edge. That helps the line-loss threshold (sum below 0.05). Mass drops from 0.7 to 0.6 kg to reduce yaw inertia and speed-tracking lag, which should help the robot follow the tighter curvature at a fixed base speed. The look-ahead stays at 0.05 m so this iteration changes only one sensing variable (spread) plus footprint, which keeps the cause of any change readable. Expected result: line_loss_events falls toward 0 on the s-bend and slalom, max_error drops below 0.06, and progress rises on the slalom and s-bend. If line losses persist at the wider spread, the next step is a longer look-ahead (y 0.07 m).",
  "geometry": {
    "sensor_positions": [
      {"x": -0.06, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": -0.03, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.03, "y": 0.05},
      {"x": 0.06, "y": 0.05}
    ],
    "sensor_size": 0.016,
    "wheel_track": 0.12,
    "wheel_radius": 0.03,
    "body_width": 0.12,
    "body_length": 0.18,
    "mass": 0.6
  },
  "intent": "Widen the sensor row from ±0.04 m to ±0.06 m and enlarge sensor footprints to 16 mm so the outer sensors see the tape earlier on sharp bends and the line is less likely to drop out. Reduce mass to 0.6 kg to lower yaw inertia. Body and wheels are unchanged so the off-track limit stays at its 0.06 m floor. Expected result: fewer line-loss events, max_error below 0.06 m, and higher progress on the slalom and s-bend.",
  "feedback_request": {
    "measurements": ["completion", "progress", "completion_time", "rms_error", "max_error", "line_loss_events", "steering_oscillation", "control_effort", "controller_errors", "noise_robustness"]
  },
  "learning": "Iteration 0 showed every run ending off track at about 0.063 m max_error with one line loss, so the failure is a line loss followed by drift rather than steady oscillation. Hypotheses for this iteration: (1) the ±0.04 m spread gives too little warning on sharp bends, so widening to ±0.06 m will cut line losses; (2) a 16 mm footprint keeps outer sensor readings above the 0.05 line-loss threshold near the tape edge; (3) lower mass improves tracking at a given speed. Ask the Integrator: on which track and bend does the first line loss occur, at what max_error, and do the outer sensors saturate? If line losses remain on the widened row, the next test is y = 0.07 m with the same spread.",
  "_presentation": {
    "entity0": {
      "label": "Wider sensor row, 16mm",
      "notes": "Widens the sensor row to ±0.06 m, enlarges footprints to 16 mm and drops mass to 0.6 kg. Every iteration-0 run ended off track at max_error about 0.063 m with one line loss, and body width cannot raise the 0.06 m limit, so the change targets sensing."
    }
  }
}
